  "solution": {
    "text": "The doubly ionized&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math> atom is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>-like atom<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>13.6</mn><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math><br /><br />The ground state energy of the doubly ionised&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math> atom<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math> will be<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>13.6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>122.4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math><br /><br />Hence, the ionisation energy for an electron in the ground state of doubly ionised&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math> atom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>122.4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>"
  }
